WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.820
Where does each atom sit?

2
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:12.020
Chemistry gives a protein's composition and sequence, not its fold.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.160
By 1991, nobody had seen the shape of the PKA catalytic subunit.

4
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.700
Shape tells you function.

5
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:29.320
In the early 1940s, biochemists had no method for molecules of thousands of atoms.

6
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.940
Kendrew judged X-ray crystallography the only one with any chance.

7
00:00:35.883 --> 00:00:38.743
Grow the pure protein into a crystal.

8
00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:44.803
Kendrew chose myoglobin because it was small, easy to prepare in quantity, and

9
00:00:44.803 --> 00:00:45.983
readily crystallized.

10
00:00:46.843 --> 00:00:50.763
Good conditions are hard to predict, so you screen many solutions.

11
00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:55.463
Inside the crystal, the molecules sit in a regular lattice.

12
00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:02.033
Keep the crystal wet and mounted, and shine a narrow beam of X-rays through it,

13
00:01:02.493 --> 00:01:03.493
rotating the crystal.

14
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:07.473
X-rays scatter from the electrons around each atom.

15
00:01:08.153 --> 00:01:10.673
In some directions the scattering adds up.

16
00:01:11.213 --> 00:01:12.753
In others it cancels.

17
00:01:13.413 --> 00:01:16.893
The crystal sends the beam into a regular pattern of spots.

18
00:01:17.693 --> 00:01:20.553
Their positions reflect how the molecules are arranged.

19
00:01:21.353 --> 00:01:24.793
Their intensities depend on the atoms inside each molecule.

20
00:01:25.783 --> 00:01:29.643
The detector records only each spot's intensity.

21
00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:32.223
The phase of each wave is lost.

22
00:01:33.123 --> 00:01:38.383
That is the phase problem: half the information needed to make the image is missing.

23
00:01:39.703 --> 00:01:45.363
Perutz and Kendrew solved it with heavy atoms, for example mercury, attached at a

24
00:01:45.363 --> 00:01:49.643
few sites. Two or more such crystals fix the phases.

25
00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:54.943
Add up all the spots as waves, and you get an electron-density map.

26
00:01:55.783 --> 00:01:58.923
Read the map as contours of density.

27
00:01:59.483 --> 00:02:02.083
Build a model into it, and refine it.

28
00:02:03.143 --> 00:02:09.063
Wide-angle spots carry the fine detail, so resolution depends on how far out the

29
00:02:09.063 --> 00:02:10.063
pattern is recorded.

30
00:02:10.983 --> 00:02:17.423
In Kendrew's myoglobin, at six angstroms, you see rods of chain and one flat disc,

31
00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:24.783
the haem. At two angstroms, helices appear as hollow cylinders and the iron sits out

32
00:02:24.783 --> 00:02:25.783
of the haem plane.

33
00:02:26.643 --> 00:02:32.543
At 1.4 angstroms, bonded neighbours are only just resolved: the limit for these

34
00:02:32.543 --> 00:02:33.543
crystals.

35
00:02:33.717 --> 00:02:40.097
No crystal, no structure: a crystal that diffracts is often the chief barrier.

36
00:02:40.917 --> 00:02:44.057
Screen many conditions, or add a binding partner.

37
00:02:44.917 --> 00:02:50.377
A map made with model phases tends to show density where the model has it, and an

38
00:02:50.377 --> 00:02:54.277
incorrect model can still refine to a fairly good R value.

39
00:02:55.237 --> 00:02:57.697
Leave about 10% of the spots out.

40
00:02:58.297 --> 00:03:01.097
Their R-free value guards against over-fitting.

41
00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:07.990
In the late 1950s, Kendrew's team built the first protein structure, myoglobin.

42
00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.810
A two-angstrom map was published in 1960.

43
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.710
Perutz compared oxygen-carrying horse haemoglobin with oxygen-free human

44
00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:20.750
haemoglobin at 5.5 angstroms.

45
00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:26.610
Two beta subunits rearranged, their separation changing by over seven angstroms.

46
00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:29.710
The two forms differ in quaternary structure.

47
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:37.920
In 1991, Knighton, Taylor, Sowadski and colleagues solved the first protein kinase

48
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.920
structure, of PKA, at 2.7 angstroms, two lobes with a cleft.

49
00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:52.680
In 1998, MacKinnon's lab solved the potassium channel KcsA at 3.2 angstroms with a

50
00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.020
selectivity filter 12 angstroms long.

51
00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:01.200
Keep this rule. A shape in a crystal is a clue, so test it in cells.
